cope for celestial navigation, and the instrument remains a backup skill in some maritime training programs today.\n  \n  WORKER_TOKEN=29537106\nagentId: ac84a208bac7601e9 (use SendMessage with to: 'ac84a208bac7601e9', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 6043\ntool_uses: 1\nduration_ms: 5970</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5494b80e-800b-49b7-99b6-f1d36a2e14c8","uuid":"a806e6c4-1c42-455e-b6b2-af94b90772d6","timestamp":"2026-11-18T11:58:14.578Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Number: 29537106. Topic: the history of the sextant.","agentId":"ac84a208bac7601e9","agentType":"worker-haiku","toolsUsed":["Bash"],"harnessNoteCount":1,"harnessTailCount":0,"harnessSectionHash":"5a89ab4152104f7d","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<tools-used-before-summary>Bash</tools-used-before-summary>\n"},{"type":"text","text":"The sextant grew out of a long search for a practical way to measure the angle between a celestial body and the horizon. Early navigators relied on the cross-staff and the back-staff, which were awkward and often required staring directly into the Sun. The back-staff, associated with John Davis around 1594, improved matters by letting the observer turn away from the Sun, but it still had limits in accuracy.\n\nThe modern sextant took shape in the 1730s. In 1731, John Hadley in England and Thomas Godfrey in Philadelphia each independently described an instrument that used two mirrors to combine a direct view of the horizon with a reflected view of the celestial body. Because the mirrors doubled the angle being measured, the instrument could read angles up to about 120 degrees while its scale covered only a 60-degree arc, roughly one sixth of a circle. That arc gave the instrument its name.\n\nThe reflecting design removed much of the guesswork from sea navigation. Later makers refined the optics, added filters to protect the eye, and improved the graduated arc and vernier scale. Sextants