{"t":9989,"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"acd2fc010fcd02f50","run_id":"0mwm2hr3z-9297af77","tool_use_id":"toolu_01KEbMarNR6kHn2i8L7qauEY","status":"completed","output_file":"C:\\Users\\spesce\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\claude\\a3af3560164d967d\\5494b80e-800b-49b7-99b6-f1d36a2e14c8\\tasks\\acd2fc010fcd02f50.output","summary":"The compass has its roots in ancient China, where people first noticed that lodestone, a naturally magnetized mineral, aligned itself north and south. The Han dynasty philosopher Wang Chong described a lodestone spoon that settled into a fixed orientation when set on a smooth plate, and it was used more for geomancy and divination than for navigation. By the Song dynasty, around the 11th century, Chinese scholars such as Shen Kuo wrote about magnetized iron needles and noticed that they did not point exactly to true north, an early observation of magnetic declination. Chinese navigators adopted magnetic needles floated on water or balanced on pivots, which made possible long sea voyages.\n\nThe compass reached Europe by the 12th century, probably through Arab and Mediterranean trade routes. Alexander Neckam, an English writer around 1190, is often cited as one of the first Western authors to describe its use at sea. European makers soon combined the needle with a rotating card marked with wind directions, creating the familiar mariner's compass with thirty-two points. In 1492, Christopher Columbus recorded variation in the compass reading across the Atlantic. In 1600, William Gilbert's treatise De Magnete argued that the Earth itself behaves like a giant magnet, offering the first scientific explanation for why compasses point north. Later refinements included liquid-damped housings, gimbals that keep the card level, and, in the twentieth century, gyrocompasses that find true north without magnetism at all.\n\nWORKER_TOKEN=61840273","usage":{"total_tokens":6140,"tool_uses":1,"duration_ms":7567},"uuid":"2b09065c-0d47-4d1b-b1ca-f8660957a397","session_id":"5494b80e-800b-49b7-99b6-f1d36a2e14c8"}
